// error: `Go` is a subtype of (() -> unit) -> unit, but its `apply` marks its parameter `f` `@sendable`, which a call through (() -> unit) -> unit would not check; the function type gives it `() -> unit`, which is not sendable: it is a function value, whose type does not say what it captures
// error: `Runner` is a subtype of (() -> unit) -> unit, but its `apply` marks its parameter `f` `@sendable`, which a call through (() -> unit) -> unit would not check; the function type gives it `() -> unit`, which is not sendable: it is a function value, whose type does not say what it captures
// error: `Keep` is a subtype of (T) -> unit, but its `apply` marks its parameter `x` `@isolated`, which a call through (T) -> unit would not check; the function type gives it `T`, which is not sendable: it is a type parameter that may stand for any type; bound it, as in `[T: Sendable]`
// error: `Cells` is a subtype of (Array[int]) -> int, but its `apply` marks its parameter `a` `@isolating`, which a call through (Array[int]) -> int would not check; the function type gives it `Array[int]`, which is not sendable: it is mutable; an immutable copy (`toVector`) is sendable
// error: `Lifter.apply` implements `Lifts.apply`, which marks its parameter `f` `@sendable` where the method it overrides does not; a call through that method would not check what is passed for it
// A value of a type that is a function is called through its function type, which carries no marks,
// so the `apply` that implements it marks a parameter only where that type makes it sendable: a data
// type's impl of a function type, a class naming one, a generic class whose parameter type is an
// unbounded `T`, and an `@isolating` array. Through a trait that declares `apply`, the override check
// reports it once.
import avin.threads.spawn

data Go(n: int)

impl (() -> unit) -> unit for Go
  apply(@sendable f: () -> unit) -> unit = spawn(f).join()

class Runner: (() -> unit) -> unit
  apply(@sendable f: () -> unit) -> unit = spawn(f).join()

class Keep[T]: (T) -> unit
  apply(@isolated x: T) -> unit = ()

class Cells: (Array[int]) -> int
  apply(@isolating a: Array[int]) -> int = a.length

trait Lifts: (() -> int) -> int
  apply(f: () -> int) -> int

class Lifter: Lifts
  override apply(@sendable f: () -> int) -> int = spawn(f).join()

main()
  val arr = Array.fill(1, 0)
  val g: (() -> unit) -> unit = Runner()
  g(() -> arr(0) = 4)
